# First real-Linux boot on hv-metal's EL2 — M5 Arc 5e, 2026-07-24.
#
# Verbatim terminal transcript, with one edit: absolute paths under the
# developer's home directory were rewritten to $BALEEN_LINUX_DIR / <repo> so this
# public repo carries no local usernames. Nothing else was altered.
#
# This is HISTORY, not a gate. The claim it makes is asserted on every CI run by
# `cargo xtask qemu-linux-test`, which checks these same markers — see
# docs/ARC-5-M5-GUEST-INTERFACE.md. It is kept because it is the only record of
# the first time this worked; the paths in it no longer exist.
#
$ dtc -I dts -O dtb $BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/guest.patched.dts -o $BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/guest.dtb
$BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/guest.patched.dts:78.3-28: Warning (clocks_property): /pl011@9000000:clocks: cell 0 is not a phandle reference
$BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/guest.patched.dts:78.3-28: Warning (clocks_property): /pl011@9000000:clocks: cell 1 is not a phandle reference
$ cargo build --release --target aarch64-unknown-none-softfloat --manifest-path hv-metal/Cargo.toml --features real-linux
   Compiling hv-metal v0.0.0 (<repo>/hv-metal)
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.33s
$ qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt,virtualization=on,gic-version=3 -cpu cortex-a72 -smp 1 -m 1024 -nographic -net none -semihosting -kernel hv-metal/target/aarch64-unknown-none-softfloat/release/hv-metal -device loader,file=$BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/Image,addr=0x48000000,force-raw=on -device loader,file=$BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/guest.dtb,addr=0x4b000000,force-raw=on -device loader,file=$BALEEN_LINUX_DIR/custom-initramfs.gz,addr=0x4c000000,force-raw=on
baleen: hv-metal alive (arc3) — the proven brain runs on the metal
baleen: CurrentEL = EL2 (running at the hypervisor level)
baleen: VBAR_EL2 installed — exception vectors live (VBAR=0x0000000040090800)
baleen: HCR_EL2.RW=1 (EL1=AArch64) — value=0x0000000080000000
baleen: generic timer live: CNTFRQ=62500000 Hz, CNTPCT 69812 -> 70187 (monotonic)
baleen: HvCall CreditGrant(100) -> balance=100 (hv-core serviced it on the metal)
baleen: M5 Arc 5e — booting a REAL aarch64 Linux kernel as a single EL1 guest (Image@0x48000000, DTB@0x4b000000, RAM 0x48000000..0x80000000)
baleen: entering EL1 — the kernel takes the machine
